# stack/autoinstall/packages.list — every package the HUB image installs.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONE PLACE THE LIST EXISTS. Four things read it and none of
# them retypes it:
#
#   vmtest/export-apt.sh          resolves this closure into a baked apt repo
#   user-data late-command 3c     `apt-get install` reads THIS FILE off the
#                                 payload at /opt/homehub/stack/autoinstall/
#   vmtest/assert-installed.sh    asserts every name here is installed, ON the
#                                 booted box
#   scripts/validate_config.py    cross-checks the runtime-dependency tables
#
# WHY IT IS NOT `packages:` ANY MORE (2026-08-06). autoinstall's `packages:`
# runs during curtin's install step, BEFORE any late-command — so no local apt
# source a late-command could add is available to it, and every entry left
# there is one more thing the install reaches for the archive to satisfy. On
# 2026-08-06 that killed a production install on the real hub: no network when
# apt ran, `cockpit` died with exit 100, subiquity errored, late-commands never
# ran, and the box rebooted as a bare Ubuntu with no sshd and a locked console
# account. Recovery took `init=/bin/bash` from GRUB.
#
# `packages:` is now EMPTY, deliberately and permanently, and everything below
# is installed from the repo baked into the payload. Putting even ONE name back
# there reintroduces the whole failure: the install would reach for the archive
# again, and it would die there first, before any of this ran.
#
# FORMAT: one package per line. `#` starts a comment, to end of line. Blank
# lines ignored. Nothing else — every reader parses it with the same three
# rules, in awk, sed, bash and python respectively.
#
# REBUILD THE BAKED REPO WHEN YOU CHANGE THIS FILE. The debs are frozen at
# export time (`bash vmtest/export-apt.sh --target hub --out .out/apt`), and a
# name added here without a re-export is a name the offline install cannot
# resolve. export-apt.sh proves the closure offline before it ships it, so the
# failure is a refused build rather than a refused install.

# ── the base the stack and the operator need ────────────────────────────────
ca-certificates
curl
git
dnsutils              # provides dig for the health check
# NAMED, NOT INHERITED. The hub has had systemd-resolved all along only because
# something heavier in this list drags it in — no line here ever asked for it.
# The wall image, with a leaner set, therefore shipped with NO RESOLVER AT ALL
# and nobody noticed until 2026-08-08 (see wall/packages.list for what that
# looked like). firstboot step 6 writes a resolved drop-in and turns its stub
# listener off so Technitium can own :53; a box where resolved is absent takes
# that drop-in and does nothing with it, silently. An implicit dependency that
# load-bearing should be explicit.
systemd-resolved      # firstboot step 6 configures it; :53 belongs to Technitium
# THE THREE BELOW ARE THE PRICE OF THE ONE ABOVE, and leaving them out cost a
# whole lab run (2026-08-08). Every binary package built from the systemd source
# Depends on systemd (= ${binary:Version}) — an EXACT match, not a floor. Naming
# systemd-resolved made export-apt.sh bake systemd 255.4-1ubuntu8.16, because
# that is what the archive had; the ISO's base system carries 8.12. apt then had
# to reconcile an 8.16 systemd with an 8.12 udev, and its only move was to
# REMOVE udev — taking initramfs-tools, cloud-init, netplan.io, mdadm,
# multipath-tools, ubuntu-minimal and ubuntu-server with it.
#
# The box came up with no /dev/disk/by-uuid/* (that is udev's
# 60-persistent-storage.rules), so /boot and /boot/efi never appeared,
# local-fs.target failed, and it reached emergency.target on every boot with no
# network at all. It looked exactly like "the hub never appeared on the network".
#
# So the rule this leaves behind: NAMING ONE PACKAGE OF A SOURCE PACKAGE'S
# BINARY FAMILY DRAGS THE WHOLE FAMILY'S VERSION, and every sibling the base
# system already has must be named beside it or apt will delete it. These three
# are the siblings Ubuntu Server installs by default. late-command 3c now passes
# --no-remove, so if this list ever falls short again the install FAILS with the
# package named instead of producing a machine that boots to a rescue prompt.
udev                  # /dev/disk/by-uuid/* — without it local-fs.target cannot resolve
systemd-timesyncd     # base-installed sibling; strict-versioned against systemd
libnss-systemd        # base-installed sibling; strict-versioned against systemd
apt-transport-https

# ── remote management (WI-10.12) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# openssh-server USED TO COME FROM `ssh: install-server: true`, which is also an
# apt install and also died on 2026-08-06 — leaving a box with a locked console
# password and no sshd, i.e. no way in at all. install-server is now `false` and
# this is the explicit, baked replacement. Do not put it back: install-server
# runs at the same pre-late-command moment `packages:` does.
openssh-server
cockpit               # LAN web console (terminal/logs/updates/reboot)
unattended-upgrades   # hands-off OS security patching

# ── power and disks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
powertop              # low-power auto-tune, applied per boot by powertune.service
hdparm                # backup-drive spin-down (WI-10.10)

# ── the household file server (A14) — shares come from storage-map §3 ───────
samba
samba-common-bin      # smbpasswd/pdbedit for the per-account passwords (A13)
# smbclient IS NOT IN samba-common-bin, and that one wrong assumption meant the
# hub's Samba surface could not be tested at all. verify-hub.sh section 7 is the
# only thing that has ever asserted the NEGATIVES here — anonymous cannot write
# to Media or Shared, anonymous cannot enter a Private tree — and every one of
# those checks skipped with "smbclient is not installed" on 2026-08-07. Two
# shares are readable with NO ACCOUNT AT ALL by ruling (Q-S5, Q-S7); the whole
# safety of that rests on the LAN-only rule and on `nobody` being in no write
# list, and the second is only a claim until something tries it.
#
# A client on a server is a fair question. The answer is that this box is the
# sole host of every storage-map section 3 share, so it is also the only place
# from which those shares can be probed with no other machine involved.
smbclient             # smbclient(1): the only way to TEST the shares this box serves
# AND cifs-utils IS NOT IN smbclient — the same shape of assumption as the one
# above, made again, and it cost two full lab runs of misdiagnosis.
#
# smbclient is a USERSPACE client. Mounting is a KERNEL filesystem, and the
# `credentials=` and name-resolution halves of it are handled by the userspace
# helper /sbin/mount.cifs, which ships in cifs-utils and NOTHING ELSE pulls in.
# Without it `mount -t cifs` still exists (the kernel module is in the stock
# image), so the failure is not "command not found" — it is far worse:
#
#   * `credentials=<file>` is never parsed, so NO credential reaches the server
#     and the session setup returns STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED;
#   * kernel-side name resolution has no upcall, so a perfectly resolvable FQDN
#     yields "Unable to determine destination address", which mount(8) prints as
#     >>> No route to host <<<.
#
# That sentence is why this took so long. "No route to host" reads as a NETWORK
# fault, and this project spent two runs and a whole handoff section concluding
# that Mini-serv's `Snapshots` share did not exist — while smbclient, sitting
# right here, could list the share and read its files the entire time. The share
# was genuinely missing at first, which made the wrong diagnosis fit; when the
# Owner created it the symptom did not move, because it had a second cause.
#
# The hub SERVES shares (samba) and TESTS them (smbclient); it must also MOUNT
# them, because storage-map §4a ingest pulls //mini-serv/Snapshots into the
# library tree and backup.sh step 1b is the only consumer of that flow.
cifs-utils            # mount.cifs: the hub MOUNTS Mini-serv's shares (storage-map §4a ingest)

# ── what backup.sh actually EXECUTES ─────────────────────────────────────────
# THE THIRD PACKAGE OF THIS EXACT SHAPE, added 2026-08-09 before it could be the
# third outage. `smbclient` was assumed to come with samba-common-bin (2026-08-07);
# `cifs-utils` was assumed to come with samba (A25, above). Both were capabilities
# taken for granted because a neighbouring package was present.
#
# rsync and zstd are the same assumption one layer up: they are not *near* a
# listed package, they are simply the binaries the backup service runs, and
# nothing had ever checked that the box has them. backup.sh calls both with no
# `command -v` guard —
#     rsync   every source kind and the step-1b ingest (backup.sh :317 :368
#             :411 :427 :464). Nothing backs up without it.
#     zstd    the compressed-archive path and its integrity test (:480 :497),
#             and restore.sh's extract. The baked repo carries libzstd1 as a
#             transitive dependency, which is the LIBRARY, not the CLI.
#
# WHY THIS WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL NOW: every backup run to date has been
# `--dry-run`, and a dry run returns before the archive step, so `zstd` had
# never been invoked on the hub even once. The first REAL backup — which is the
# next thing planned — is where it would have surfaced, on a box with no route
# to the archive to fix itself.
rsync                 # the transport for every backup source kind, and for ingest
zstd                  # the .tar.zst archive path, its integrity test, and restore

# ── what a FileBackup RESTORE needs, on a box with no FileBackup ─────────────
# These three are not for the backup — the container carries everything the
# backup run uses. They are for the RECONSTRUCTION, and that is the whole point
# of naming them here (E2 / P1.6): the standing premise of the "avoided where
# possible" ruling is that the archive stays restorable WITHOUT its own runtime.
# `bash/reconstruct.sh` ships inside every snapshot and runs on plain coreutils
# plus these; if they are absent, the recovery half of the contract needs the
# very container whose absence is the likely reason someone is reconstructing.
#
# GAWK IS NAMED SEPARATELY BECAUSE UBUNTU SERVER SHIPS MAWK. reconstruct.sh
# parses MANIFEST.csv with `FPAT`, which is a GNU awk extension: under mawk the
# field splitting is simply wrong on any quoted field containing a comma — it
# does not error, it restores the wrong paths. An alternative that silently
# mis-parses is worse than one that is missing.
xxhash                # xxh128sum: the manifest's hash algorithm (verify + drill oracles)
gawk                  # reconstruct.sh needs FPAT — Ubuntu's default awk is MAWK
p7zip-full            # 7z: extracts the compressed data files reconstruct.sh finds

# ── the Docker engine ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THESE COME FROM download.docker.com, NOT THE UBUNTU ARCHIVE, and that is the
# one thing about this list a reader has to know. export-apt.sh notices
# `docker-ce` here and adds Docker's apt source to its resolver container for
# the duration of the resolve; the resulting debs are baked exactly like the
# archive ones, so the INSTALL needs neither the archive nor Docker's CDN.
#
# They used to be installed by three late-commands that fetched Docker's GPG key
# and wrote /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list on the target. Those are gone.
# The absence of /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc on the 2026-08-06 box is what
# located that failure EARLIER than late-commands — a diagnosis this list makes
# permanently unavailable, because the key is no longer part of an install.
docker-ce
docker-ce-cli
containerd.io
docker-buildx-plugin
docker-compose-plugin

# ── the IceDrive GUI AppImage's RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES ────────────────────────
# THE APPIMAGE COULD NEVER HAVE RUN ON A STOCK HUB, and nobody had found out
# because it had never been launched on one. Measured on the bench box
# 2026-08-27 by installing it and following the failures one at a time:
#
#   libwebpmux.so.3      -> the binary aborts before Qt starts
#   libwebpdemux.so.2       "
#   libXss.so.1             "
#   libxcb-icccm.so.4    -> "Could not load the Qt platform plugin xcb ...
#   libxcb-image.so.0        even though it was found", then a core dump.
#   libxcb-keysyms.so.1      Eight more, one per launch, each hidden behind
#   libxcb-render-util.so.0  the one in front of it.
#   libxcb-shape.so.0
#   libxcb-xinerama.so.0
#   libxcb-xkb.so.1
#   libxkbcommon-x11.so.0
#
# The AppImage bundles Qt but NOT the system libraries Qt's xcb platform plugin
# links against, and the failure names a plugin rather than a package - so it
# reads like a corrupt download rather than a missing dependency.
#
# THIS IS THE SAME CLASS THE WALL IMAGE ALREADY GUARDS: validate_config.py
# checks "every Electron runtime package is in the wall image (27 sonames ->
# 23 packages)". The hub had no equivalent check for its one AppImage, which is
# why an untested claim survived. Adding one is open work.
libwebpmux3
libwebpdemux2
libxss1
libxcb-icccm4
libxcb-image0
libxcb-keysyms1
libxcb-render-util0
libxcb-shape0
libxcb-xinerama0
libxcb-xkb1
libxkbcommon-x11-0

# ── the FUSE 2 shim, for the IceDrive CLI ───────────────────────────────────
# Ubuntu 24.04 ships libfuse3; IcedriveCLI is a native ELF linked against
# libfuse.so.2 and will not start without this. Measured 2026-08-27: `ldd` on
# the CLI reports `libfuse.so.2 => not found` on a stock 24.04 and resolves
# once this package is present.
#
# THIS USED TO BE HERE FOR THE APPIMAGE, and the AppImage is gone (see below).
# It stays because the CLI — the thing that replaced it — needs the same shim
# for its FUSE mount. Do not remove it with the rest of the graphical set.
libfuse2t64

# ── the graphical session (SR-015) IS NOT INSTALLED — see packages.optional.list
# RULED BY THE OWNER 2026-08-27: remove the GUI from the image and from the box,
# along with the boot-time desktop session. The nine names that used to be
# listed here moved to packages.optional.list, which bakes them into the offline
# repo WITHOUT installing them — so `HomeHubDesktop.cmd` can still turn a desktop
# on later, on a box with no internet, and nothing here starts one.
#
# WHY IT WENT. The layer existed for exactly one app: IceDrive, believed to be
# GUI-only. It is not — `IcedriveCLI` is a headless native ELF that logs in
# non-interactively and FUSE-mounts the cloud with no X server at all (measured
# 2026-08-27; see stack/icedrive/README.md). With its only tenant gone, an
# always-listening tcp/3389, an XFCE session and an unattended auto-login
# session were cost without a purpose.
#
# This supersedes the 2026-08-26 ruling that defaulted the layer ON, and
# restores SN-012's shape — an opt-in nobody has turned on — with SN-001's
# zero-click core no longer carrying any exception at all.
